{"text": " The AI component is a yellow pose model that estimates the athletes' visible, body, landmarks, frame by frame. However, the AI does not choose the athlete or interpret what the injury means. Instead, a researcher identifies the player, defies the movement window and visually reviews the tracking. Every pose estimates then passes through confidence and reliability checks. Unsupported frames remain missing rather than being artificially competed. The supported Latin marks become projected to the movement measurements while deterministic rules identify sustained changes and organize them into a movement story. This separation is our responsible AI design. AI extracts observable movement, the human retains contextual judgment, and evidence gates control what the system is allowed to report. Every finding links back to its source frame. When the evidence is insufficient, the result is narrowed or withheld rather than guessed. This responsible behavior is visible in the application and not just hidden in a disclaimer. Depending on the evidence, the system can produce a complete movement story, limit the analysis to a supported interval, report the no-sustained transition was detected or withhold face analysis entirely. It never uses missing evidence to manufacture a certainty and it never presents movement similarity as a diagnosis or injury prediction. Sometimes the most responsible answer is less information.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 5.6, "text": " The AI component is a yellow pose model that estimates the athletes' visible, body,", "tokens": [50363, 383, 9552, 7515, 318, 257, 7872, 12705, 2746, 326, 7746, 262, 10856, 6, 7424, 11, 1767, 11, 50637], "temperature": 0, "avg_logprob": -0.3098258052963808, "compression_ratio": 1.5738396624472575, "no_speech_prob": 0.019283846020698547, "words": [{"word": " The", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.2, "probability": 0.83642578125}, {"word": " AI", "start": 0.2, "end": 0.58, "probability": 0.96777